.\" Manual page for quickkey.
.TH QUICKKEY 1 "2026-10-25" "quickkey 1.3.0" "User Commands"
.
.SH NAME
quickkey \- pop up a menu of commands and copy the output of the one you pick
.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.SY quickkey
.OP \-c file
.OP \-k
.OP \-t seconds
.OP \-\-no\-strip
.OP \-\-selection both|clipboard|primary
.OP \-\-monitor active|primary|all
.OP \-\-daemon
.OP \-\-no\-daemon
.OP \-\-list
.OP \-\-title title
.YS
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B quickkey
(said like \[lq]quickie\[rq]) reads a config file of named commands, shows them
in a small window you can drive from the keyboard or the mouse, and puts the
standard output of whichever one you choose onto the clipboard.  Then it gets
out of the way.
.PP
It is meant to be bound to a hotkey, so it is built for the hundred
milliseconds after that key press rather than for a long session: no
splash, no confirmation you have to dismiss, and no startup work that can be
deferred.  See
.B SPEED
below for the resident daemon, which cuts the whole round trip to about 20ms.
.PP
Typing filters the list.  A bare digit ranks the entry at that position in the
config first, so
.B 4
then
.B Return
runs the fourth entry whatever it is called.  The numbers shown are config
positions and never change as you filter, so the one you reach for is the one
you saw.
.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-c " file" "\fR, \fP" \-\-config " file"
Read
.I file
instead of searching the usual places.  It is an error if it does not exist.
.TP
.B "\-k, \-\-keep\-open"
Stay open after copying, and report what was copied in the status line,
instead of exiting.  Useful for grabbing several things in a row.
.TP
.BI \-t " seconds" "\fR, \fP" \-\-timeout " seconds"
Give a command this long to finish (default 30).
.B 0
removes the limit.  A command that runs over is killed and reported; nothing
is copied.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-strip
Keep the leading and trailing newlines of the output.  By default they are
stripped, since a command substitution would have dropped the final one
anyway and a stray newline in a paste is rarely what you want.
.TP
.BI \-\-selection " both" "\fR|\fP" clipboard "\fR|\fP" primary
Which X selection to write (default
.BR both ).
.B CLIPBOARD
is what \fBCtrl\-V\fP pastes;
.B PRIMARY
is what middle\-click and \fBShift\-Insert\fP paste, which is what
.BR urxvt (1)
and
.BR xterm (1)
use.  Writing only one of them is a good way to be surprised by an
application that reads the other.
.TP
.BI \-\-monitor " active" "\fR|\fP" primary "\fR|\fP" all
Which monitor to appear on.
.B active
(default) follows the mouse pointer,
.B primary
always uses the monitor X marks primary, and
.B all
centres across the whole display \- correct on one monitor, and on several it
lands near the seam.  Layout comes from
.BR xrandr (1),
cached so that it never runs on the path to showing the window.
.TP
.B \-\-daemon
Stay resident, holding a built window, and pop it up when a later bare
.B quickkey
wakes it.  See
.BR SPEED .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-daemon
Draw a fresh window even when a daemon is running.  Any option at all has
this effect, since the resident window was built with the daemon's options
and honouring new ones would mean silently ignoring them.
.TP
.B \-\-list
Print the configured names and commands, then exit.  Does not need a display.
.TP
.BI \-\-title " title"
Set the window title, which is how a window manager rule can recognise the
window.
.
.SH KEYS
.TP
.I text
Filter the list.  A bare digit also ranks that config position first.
.TP
.B "Return, KP_Enter"
Run the selected entry.
.TP
.BR Alt\- \fIN\fP
Run the entry labelled
.IR N ,
1 to 9, if the filter still shows it.
.TP
.B "Up, Down, Ctrl\-P, Ctrl\-N"
Move the selection.
.TP
.B "PageUp, PageDown"
Move ten rows.
.TP
.B "Home, End"
First or last entry.
.TP
.B "Escape, Ctrl\-C"
Dismiss.  With a daemon this hides the window; otherwise it exits.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-Q
Quit for real, ending the daemon too.
.PP
A single click selects, a double click runs.
.
.SH CONFIG
An INI file.  Each
.B [section]
is a name in the menu, and its
.B command
is run with the shell:
.PP
.EX
[Public IP]
command = curl \-s https://ifconfig.me

[UUID]
command = cat /proc/sys/kernel/random/uuid
.EE
.PP
A command ending in
.B &
is launched and forgotten: the window closes at once, nothing is waited for,
and nothing is copied.  Use it for anything long\-lived, which would otherwise
hold the window open until it exited.
.PP
.EX
[LibreOffice]
command = libreoffice &
.EE
.PP
An interactive screen\-capture tool works as a background entry, but mind what
its selection gesture means.
.BR scrot (1)
.B \-s
takes a rectangle when you
.I drag,
and the window under the pointer when you merely
.I click
\- and once this window has closed, the thing under the pointer is usually the
desktop, which is the whole screen.  Drag, or move onto the window you want
first.
.PP
The file is re\-read when it changes, so a daemon picks up edits without a
restart.
.
.SH SPEED
A cold run is about 58ms, nearly all of it Tk building the window.  Running
one copy as a daemon moves that cost off the hotkey path: the resident
process already has the window built, and a bare
.B quickkey
then only connects to a socket and exits, without importing
.B tkinter
at all \- about 20ms from key press to window.
.PP
A systemd user unit ships with the package:
.PP
.EX
systemctl \-\-user enable \-\-now quickkey
.EE
.PP
Bind the hotkey to a plain
.B quickkey
with no arguments.  That is the only form that hands off to the daemon.
.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/quickkey/config
The config file, tried first (then
.IR config.ini ,
then
.IR ~/.quickkeyrc ,
then a
.I quickkey.conf
beside the script, then
.IR /etc/quickkey/config ,
then
.IR /usr/share/quickkey/quickkey.conf ).
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/quickkey.sock
Where the daemon listens, mode 0600.  Falls back to
.I /tmp/quickkey\-$UID.sock
when that path is too long for a Unix socket.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/quickkey\-monitors
Cached monitor layout.  Safe to delete; it is rebuilt in the background.
.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B QUICKKEY_CONFIG
Config file to use.  When set, no other location is tried.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Base for the default config path, defaulting to
.IR ~/.config .
.TP
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
Where the socket and the monitor cache live.
.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The output was copied, or the window was dismissed without choosing anything.
.TP
.B 1
A command failed, timed out, or could not be run, or the clipboard could not
be written.  The error stays on screen rather than flashing past, so the
window does not close.
.TP
.B 2
Bad usage, or no config file was found.
.
.SH DEPENDENCIES
Python 3.10 or newer with
.BR tkinter ,
which several distributions package separately
.RB ( python3\-tk
on Debian,
.B python3\-tkinter
on Fedora,
.B tk
on Arch).  Copying needs one of
.BR xclip (1),
.BR xsel (1),
.BR wl\-copy (1),
.BR pbcopy (1)
or
.BR clip.exe ;
Tk's own clipboard is not enough, because an X selection belongs to a process
and dies with it.  Multi\-monitor placement uses
.BR xrandr (1)
when it is installed.
.
.SH EXAMPLES
Run it the way a hotkey should, handing off to the daemon if one is up:
.PP
.EX
quickkey
.EE
.PP
Grab several values in one visit, keeping only
.BR PRIMARY :
.PP
.EX
quickkey \-k \-\-selection primary
.EE
.PP
Check a config without a display:
.PP
.EX
QUICKKEY_CONFIG=./quickkey.conf quickkey \-\-list
.EE
.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR xclip (1),
.BR xsel (1),
.BR wl\-copy (1),
.BR xrandr (1),
.BR systemd.unit (5)
.PP
